  "query_preview": "..\nA strong Southwest wind at the surface has been transporting\nmoisture into northeast Montana for the last several days and will\npersist for yet another evening. With a strong ridge of hotter\ntemps aloft and a lack of mixing during the day an inversion is\nsetting up over the region again an again each night. The \ninversion, coupled with the moisture, will result in fog and low \nstratus across eastern portions of the state again tonight.\n\nFog aside, an upper-level ridge over Montana will result in \nwarmer than average temperatures through Monday.\n\nThe ridge aloft will eventually exit eastward ahead of an upper-\nlevel trough that that will set over the Pacific Northwest, \nresulting in SW flow aloft for more normal temps and increasing \nchances of precipitation beginning Tuesday through the week with\nWednesday standing out as a particularly higher chance (30-60%). \nAt this time, the predominant mode is expected to be rain as \nopposed to snow but we re very close to freezing and this could \neasily switch in future updates.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n- Changes from NBM were to add fog to all counties other than \n  Garfield and Petroleum at various points tonight. Though Dense \n  fog does not look likely along main roads or cities. \n\n- Confidence in fog placement Sunday morning is moderate (60%).\n  Confidence in intensity is closer to (30%).\n \n- Confidence in fair conditions persisting through Monday is high\n  (90%).\n\n- Confidence in early-to-mid-Week pattern change is moderate to\n  high (83%).\n\n- Confidence in precipitation mode and amounts next week is low\n  (20%).\n\nDON/GAH",
